WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.582
Welcome to episode six.

00:00:01.622 --> 00:00:07.433
We've used combustion equations all along;
now we'll learn to write them ourselves,

00:00:07.473 --> 00:00:12.126
including the messy cases, and
track exactly which gases come out.

00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:13.681
Two quick checks.

00:00:13.721 --> 00:00:17.888
In the reaction two hydrogen
plus oxygen gives two water,

00:00:17.928 --> 00:00:22.611
how much water forms from zero
point three zero moles of oxygen?

00:00:22.651 --> 00:00:26.375
And does burning biomethane
produce carbon dioxide?

00:00:26.415 --> 00:00:27.670
Pause and answer.

00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:41.426
Oxygen to water is one to two, so
zero point six zero moles of water.

00:00:41.466 --> 00:00:47.763
And yes, burning biomethane produces carbon
dioxide, one mole per mole of methane,

00:00:47.803 --> 00:00:49.735
just like fossil methane.

00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:54.958
In complete combustion, a fuel
burns in plenty of oxygen,

00:00:54.998 --> 00:01:00.571
and all its carbon ends up as carbon
dioxide and all its hydrogen as water.

00:01:00.611 --> 00:01:04.312
Let's balance the complete
combustion of methanol,

00:01:04.352 --> 00:01:07.645
a fuel that already
contains an oxygen atom.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.564
Balance carbon first.

00:01:09.604 --> 00:01:13.765
Methanol has one carbon, so
we need one carbon dioxide.

00:01:13.805 --> 00:01:16.402
The counters now match for carbon.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:17.972
Next, hydrogen.

00:01:18.012 --> 00:01:24.116
Methanol has four hydrogen atoms: three
on the carbon and one on the oxygen.

00:01:24.156 --> 00:01:28.278
Each water has two, so we
need two water molecules.

00:01:28.666 --> 00:01:34.386
Oxygen last, because oxygen gas is the
only reactant we can adjust freely.

00:01:34.426 --> 00:01:40.458
The products now contain two oxygens in
carbon dioxide plus two in the waters:

00:01:40.498 --> 00:01:41.547
four in total.

00:01:41.587 --> 00:01:44.505
Methanol already supplies one of them.

00:01:44.545 --> 00:01:51.084
So oxygen gas must supply the other three,
which is one and a half oxygen molecules.

00:01:51.466 --> 00:01:56.405
A half is fine in a thermochemical
equation, but to get whole numbers,

00:01:56.445 --> 00:02:02.025
double everything: two methanol plus
three oxygen gives two carbon dioxide plus

00:02:02.065 --> 00:02:02.807
four water.

00:02:02.847 --> 00:02:08.324
Check the counters: two carbons, eight
hydrogens, eight oxygens on each side.

00:02:09.266 --> 00:02:12.463
Same routine for octane,
a component of petrol.

00:02:12.503 --> 00:02:15.218
Eight carbons give eight carbon dioxide.

00:02:15.258 --> 00:02:17.668
Eighteen hydrogens give nine water.

00:02:18.066 --> 00:02:23.319
Oxygen in the products: sixteen in the
carbon dioxide plus nine in the water,

00:02:23.359 --> 00:02:24.556
twenty-five atoms.

00:02:24.596 --> 00:02:29.162
Octane contains no oxygen, so all
twenty-five come from oxygen gas:

00:02:29.202 --> 00:02:31.087
twelve and a half molecules.

00:02:31.127 --> 00:02:36.586
Doubling gives two octane plus twenty-five
oxygen forming sixteen carbon dioxide

00:02:36.626 --> 00:02:38.001
plus eighteen water.

00:02:38.999 --> 00:02:43.167
When oxygen is limited,
combustion can be incomplete.

00:02:43.207 --> 00:02:49.440
Some of the carbon ends up as carbon
monoxide, a toxic gas, or as solid carbon,

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.107
which we see as soot.

00:02:51.147 --> 00:02:53.608
The hydrogen still forms water.

00:02:53.999 --> 00:02:57.408
Here are three balanced
equations for methane.

00:02:57.448 --> 00:03:02.355
Complete combustion needs two moles
of oxygen per mole of methane.

00:03:02.395 --> 00:03:05.954
Burning to carbon monoxide
needs one and a half.

00:03:05.994 --> 00:03:08.278
Burning to soot needs only one.

00:03:08.318 --> 00:03:12.401
Less oxygen per mole of fuel,
and less energy released,

00:03:12.441 --> 00:03:15.589
because the carbon is less fully oxidised.

00:03:15.933 --> 00:03:17.832
But here's the subtle point.

00:03:17.872 --> 00:03:22.265
Saying "limited oxygen" doesn't
tell you which of these happens.

00:03:22.305 --> 00:03:27.875
A real flame usually makes a mixture of
carbon dioxide, carbon monoxide and soot,

00:03:27.915 --> 00:03:30.992
in proportions that
depend on the conditions.

00:03:31.032 --> 00:03:36.118
So you can only write an incomplete-combustion
equation when the products,

00:03:36.158 --> 00:03:38.997
or enough data about them, are specified.

00:03:39.333 --> 00:03:44.656
What the amounts can tell you is
whether combustion must be incomplete.

00:03:44.696 --> 00:03:50.698
Suppose one point zero zero mole of methane
has only one point six zero moles of

00:03:50.738 --> 00:03:51.227
oxygen.

00:03:51.267 --> 00:03:57.345
Complete combustion would need two point
zero zero, so there isn't enough oxygen,

00:03:57.385 --> 00:03:59.913
and combustion must be incomplete.

00:03:59.953 --> 00:04:02.859
If you're also told that no soot forms,

00:04:02.899 --> 00:04:06.409
the extra carbon must
leave as carbon monoxide:

00:04:06.449 --> 00:04:12.301
zero point two zero moles of carbon
dioxide and zero point eight zero moles of

00:04:12.341 --> 00:04:16.646
carbon monoxide, with two
point zero zero moles of water.

00:04:17.599 --> 00:04:21.266
Practice question Q eleven
is worth five marks.

00:04:21.306 --> 00:04:23.752
Pause the video and attempt it.

00:04:36.199 --> 00:04:42.329
We're told everything we need: one mole of
ethanol and two point five zero moles of

00:04:42.369 --> 00:04:47.235
oxygen react completely, and the
only products are carbon dioxide,

00:04:47.275 --> 00:04:49.688
carbon monoxide and water vapour.

00:04:49.728 --> 00:04:54.372
So write the skeleton with
unknowns: x moles of carbon dioxide,

00:04:54.412 --> 00:04:57.608
y moles of carbon
monoxide, and some water.

00:04:57.999 --> 00:04:59.112
Hydrogen first.

00:04:59.152 --> 00:05:04.152
Ethanol has six hydrogen atoms,
so it forms three moles of water.

00:05:04.532 --> 00:05:05.557
Carbon next.

00:05:05.597 --> 00:05:09.857
Ethanol has two carbons,
so x plus y equals two.

00:05:10.199 --> 00:05:15.463
Now oxygen, and here's where the
ethanol's own oxygen matters.

00:05:15.503 --> 00:05:22.479
Oxygen atoms going in: one from ethanol
plus five from the two and a half moles of

00:05:22.519 --> 00:05:24.104
oxygen gas, so six.

00:05:24.144 --> 00:05:29.494
Coming out: two x in carbon
dioxide, plus y in carbon monoxide,

00:05:29.534 --> 00:05:31.548
plus three in the water.

00:05:31.588 --> 00:05:38.175
So two x plus y plus three equals six,
which means two x plus y equals three.

00:05:38.532 --> 00:05:42.816
Subtract the carbon equation
from that: x equals one.

00:05:42.856 --> 00:05:44.284
Then y equals one.

00:05:44.324 --> 00:05:51.055
So one mole of carbon dioxide, one mole of
carbon monoxide and three moles of water

00:05:51.095 --> 00:05:51.895
vapour.

00:05:52.065 --> 00:05:58.079
The balanced equation: ethanol plus two
and a half oxygen forms carbon dioxide plus

00:05:58.119 --> 00:06:00.997
carbon monoxide plus three water vapour.

00:06:01.037 --> 00:06:06.322
Complete combustion would need three
moles of oxygen per mole of ethanol,

00:06:06.362 --> 00:06:09.571
so this reaction had
half a mole too little.

00:06:09.932 --> 00:06:15.617
Marks: one for the hydrogen balance,
two for the carbon and oxygen balances,

00:06:15.657 --> 00:06:19.308
one for the equation, and
one for the comparison.

00:06:19.348 --> 00:06:20.513
Here's the trap.

00:06:20.553 --> 00:06:23.526
If you forget the oxygen inside ethanol,

00:06:23.566 --> 00:06:27.368
the oxygen balance becomes
two x plus y equals two,

00:06:27.408 --> 00:06:32.038
and you'd conclude that all the
carbon became carbon monoxide.

00:06:32.078 --> 00:06:36.332
That looks like the textbook
answer for "limited oxygen",

00:06:36.372 --> 00:06:38.556
but it's wrong for this data.

00:06:39.531 --> 00:06:42.282
Now think about the gases themselves.

00:06:42.322 --> 00:06:45.979
In a hot exhaust, well
above one hundred degrees,

00:06:46.019 --> 00:06:50.052
the water formed by combustion
is a gas: water vapour.

00:06:50.092 --> 00:06:55.975
Alongside it are carbon dioxide, nitrogen
from the air, and any excess oxygen.

00:06:56.331 --> 00:06:59.885
If the exhaust is cooled
to room temperature,

00:06:59.925 --> 00:07:05.476
most of the water vapour condenses
to liquid water and can be removed.

00:07:05.516 --> 00:07:07.952
What remains is called dry gas.

00:07:07.992 --> 00:07:13.423
Carbon dioxide stays a gas, so it
can now be collected and measured.

00:07:13.798 --> 00:07:15.903
So keep two accounting columns.

00:07:15.943 --> 00:07:19.986
One lists what the reaction
formed, including water vapour.

00:07:20.026 --> 00:07:24.622
The other lists what is actually
measured after cooling and drying.

00:07:24.662 --> 00:07:29.950
A question about greenhouse gases in a
hot exhaust includes the water vapour;

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:35.388
a question about a dry gas measured at
standard laboratory conditions doesn't.

00:07:35.731 --> 00:07:38.888
And gas volumes always
need their conditions.

00:07:38.928 --> 00:07:44.430
Twenty-four point eight litres per mole
applies at twenty-five degrees and one

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:45.851
hundred kilopascals.

00:07:45.891 --> 00:07:51.534
A hot gas at six hundred degrees occupies
roughly three times as much volume per

00:07:51.574 --> 00:07:56.547
mole, so you can't apply the standard
molar volume to the hot exhaust.

00:07:57.530 --> 00:08:01.209
Practice question Q twelve
is worth five marks.

00:08:01.249 --> 00:08:04.097
Pause the video and work through it.

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:25.539
The equation: methane plus two oxygen
forms carbon dioxide plus two water,

00:08:25.579 --> 00:08:29.380
with the water as a gas
at six hundred degrees.

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:35.592
Methane to carbon dioxide is one to one,
and methane to water is one to two.

00:08:35.632 --> 00:08:42.213
So zero point two five zero moles of
methane forms zero point two five zero moles

00:08:42.253 --> 00:08:47.974
of carbon dioxide and zero point five
zero zero moles of water vapour.

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:50.170
Masses in the hot stream.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:56.035
Carbon dioxide: zero point two five zero
times forty-four point zero is eleven

00:08:56.075 --> 00:08:57.314
point zero grams.

00:08:57.354 --> 00:09:03.480
Water: zero point five zero zero times
eighteen point zero is nine point zero zero

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:03.931
grams.

00:09:03.971 --> 00:09:08.858
Together, twenty point zero grams
of newly formed greenhouse gas.

00:09:09.197 --> 00:09:13.521
After cooling and drying, only
the carbon dioxide is measured,

00:09:13.561 --> 00:09:16.195
now at standard laboratory conditions:

00:09:16.235 --> 00:09:22.037
zero point two five zero moles times
twenty-four point eight litres per mole is six

00:09:22.077 --> 00:09:23.626
point two zero litres.

00:09:23.997 --> 00:09:29.505
Marks: one for the mole ratios, two
for the masses, one for the total,

00:09:29.545 --> 00:09:31.328
and one for the volume.

00:09:31.368 --> 00:09:37.907
The traps: leaving out the hot water vapour,
giving eleven grams instead of twenty,

00:09:37.947 --> 00:09:43.337
or using twenty-four point eight
for the gas at six hundred degrees.

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:49.394
Changed condition: if the same carbon
dioxide were measured while still at six

00:09:49.434 --> 00:09:52.611
hundred degrees and one
hundred kilopascals,

00:09:52.651 --> 00:09:57.331
would its volume be more or less
than six point two zero litres?

00:10:02.797 --> 00:10:03.151
More.

00:10:03.191 --> 00:10:06.779
At a higher temperature
and the same pressure,

00:10:06.819 --> 00:10:10.605
the same amount of gas
occupies a larger volume.

00:10:11.596 --> 00:10:12.412
Checkpoint.

00:10:12.452 --> 00:10:18.009
Methane combustion forms twice as many
moles of water as carbon dioxide.

00:10:18.049 --> 00:10:20.886
Does it form twice the mass of water?

00:10:20.926 --> 00:10:22.247
Pause and decide.

00:10:28.663 --> 00:10:28.854
No.

00:10:28.894 --> 00:10:34.087
Per mole of methane: one mole of
carbon dioxide is forty-four grams,

00:10:34.127 --> 00:10:37.396
and two moles of water
is thirty-six grams.

00:10:37.436 --> 00:10:43.014
Twice the moles, but less mass, because
a water molecule is much lighter.

00:10:43.054 --> 00:10:45.323
Coefficients give mole ratios.

00:10:45.363 --> 00:10:48.364
Masses also depend on the molar masses.

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:50.081
One more distinction.

00:10:50.121 --> 00:10:55.578
"Carbon emissions" usually means carbon
dioxide, or the mass of carbon it contains.

00:10:55.618 --> 00:11:00.677
"Greenhouse gases" is broader: carbon
dioxide, water vapour, unburnt methane,

00:11:00.717 --> 00:11:01.406
and others.

00:11:01.446 --> 00:11:06.373
So always check which gases a question's
model counts before you add masses

00:11:06.413 --> 00:11:07.213
together.

00:11:07.996 --> 00:11:08.801
Checkpoint.

00:11:08.841 --> 00:11:14.866
Write a balanced equation, with states,
for the complete combustion of propane,

00:11:14.906 --> 00:11:16.708
C three H eight, at SLC.

00:11:16.748 --> 00:11:18.053
Pause and try it.

00:11:30.463 --> 00:11:35.045
Carbon first: three carbon
atoms make three carbon dioxide.

00:11:35.085 --> 00:11:39.119
Then hydrogen: eight hydrogen
atoms make four water.

00:11:39.159 --> 00:11:44.994
Then oxygen: six atoms in the carbon
dioxide plus four in the water is ten,

00:11:45.034 --> 00:11:47.423
which is five oxygen molecules.

00:11:47.463 --> 00:11:53.925
So propane gas plus five oxygen gas forms
three carbon dioxide gas plus four water,

00:11:53.965 --> 00:11:56.550
and at SLC the water is a liquid.

00:11:57.529 --> 00:11:58.098
To recap.

00:11:58.138 --> 00:12:01.951
Balance combustion in the
order carbon, hydrogen, oxygen,

00:12:01.991 --> 00:12:04.655
and remember oxygen already in the fuel.

00:12:04.695 --> 00:12:09.048
Write incomplete combustion only
when the products are specified.

00:12:09.088 --> 00:12:11.549
Use states that match the conditions,

00:12:11.589 --> 00:12:16.456
and separate what a reaction formed
from what is measured after cooling.

00:12:16.796 --> 00:12:17.821
Closing recall.

00:12:17.861 --> 00:12:22.122
Balance the complete combustion
of propane, C three H eight.

00:12:32.529 --> 00:12:35.327
Three carbons give three carbon dioxide.

00:12:35.367 --> 00:12:37.598
Eight hydrogens give four water.

00:12:37.638 --> 00:12:42.281
That's ten oxygen atoms in the
products, so five oxygen molecules.

00:12:42.321 --> 00:12:47.105
Propane plus five oxygen forms three
carbon dioxide plus four water.

00:12:47.145 --> 00:12:51.403
Next episode: what happens when
one reactant runs out first.
